# Phase 4 decided that ALL THREE clauses of `for init; cond; post:` are
# required, and that bare `for:` is the only degenerate form. That is not a rule
# bolted onto the grammar -- it is what the grammar already said: this parser has
# no empty-statement production, so an empty init or post clause has nothing to
# parse, and an empty condition has no `bool` to type-check. Each is refused by
# name rather than by whatever the statement parser happened to say next:
#
#     for ; i < 3; i += 1:      the init clause is required -- write `for:` for an infinite loop
#     for i := 0; ; i += 1:     the condition clause is required -- write `for:` for an infinite loop
#     for i := 0; i < 3; :      the post clause is required -- write `for cond:` for a loop that advances in its body
#
# This fixture carries the first. (The reject lane asserts a loud refusal, not
# the message text -- the message is asserted by this comment and by the
# evidence block under phase 4 in plan.md.)
fn main():
    i := 0
    for ; i < 3; i += 1:
        println(str(i))
